{
  "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8092-b7d5-d6529d830de8",
  "title": "Entity Mapping with JPA Annotations (@Entity, @Id, @GeneratedValue, @OneToMany, etc.)",
  "sourceUrl": "https://xpandeyed.notion.site/20924eb1ed548092b7d5d6529d830de8",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80c6-b416-d0e8bff9be8b",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-803c-b0ea-c2b217489492",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "✅ 1. Core Concept (2-3 sentences)"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8094-b37c-cfa7025a7793",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Entity Mapping",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " in Spring Boot using "
        ],
        [
          "JPA (Java Persistence API)",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " lets you define how Java objects (like classes) are stored in a database. It bridges the gap between the "
        ],
        [
          "object-oriented world (Java classes)",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " and the "
        ],
        [
          "relational world (database tables)",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " by using annotations like "
        ],
        [
          "@Entity",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", "
        ],
        [
          "@Id",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", "
        ],
        [
          "@OneToMany",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", etc. This allows you to treat your database tables like regular Java objects with properties and methods."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80bd-b9d0-ed1175fe10b8",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80a7-a99b-dedc6339b28b",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "🎯 2. Key Interview Points"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80bc-8df6-edceb115cebe",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What is an ",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "@Entity",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ],
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " — A class annotated with "
        ],
        [
          "@Entity",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " is a JPA-managed persistent object that maps to a database table."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-806d-8321-f22c0f9e0701",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "How does ",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "@Id",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ],
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " and ",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "@GeneratedValue",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ],
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " work?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " — These specify the primary key and how its value is generated (e.g., auto-increment)."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8099-837b-ca71d8ece80b",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "How do you map relationships?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " — Use "
        ],
        [
          "@OneToMany",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", "
        ],
        [
          "@ManyToOne",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", etc., to represent real-world relationships like “a user has many orders”."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8046-a84f-fd189990efd3",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Lazy vs Eager loading",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " — Common performance topic around how related entities are fetched (all at once or when accessed)."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8085-91e9-f2e97d658c77",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80ca-81e2-f762e1ad6420",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "💻 3. Code Example"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8043-97f4-fc675b5bb569",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Let's model a "
        ],
        [
          "User",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " who can have "
        ],
        [
          "multiple posts",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " — a common blog application use case."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8068-a1ae-e618460af3dc",
      "type": "code",
      "richText": [
        [
          "import jakarta.persistence.*;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n@Entity\npublic class User {\n\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)\n    private Long id;  // Primary key\n\n    private String name;\n\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"user\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)\n    private List<Post> posts;\n\n    // Getters and Setters\n}\n\n@Entity\npublic class Post {\n\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)\n    private Long id;  // Primary key\n\n    private String title;\n\n    @ManyToOne\n    @JoinColumn(name = \"user_id\")\n    private User user;\n\n    // Getters and Setters\n}\n"
        ]
      ],
      "language": "Java"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8071-85d6-dcf2639563f2",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What's happening here?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-801d-8585-c59dee853c09",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "@Entity",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Both classes are mapped to database tables."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8001-a793-d1424b38bd60",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "@Id",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Marks the primary key."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8099-9d79-ecad0e47617f",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "@GeneratedValue",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Auto-generates the ID value."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-808c-b2dc-f283618d29bf",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "@OneToMany",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " and "
        ],
        [
          "@ManyToOne",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Define the relationship — a user has many posts, but each post belongs to one user."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8088-a836-c0e943bdd53b",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "mappedBy = \"user\"",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Tells JPA the "
        ],
        [
          "Post",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " side owns the relationship."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8028-bbf7-ec7c041e3efa",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8094-a2dc-e28d57def4e8",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "❓ 4. Common Interview Questions"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8003-993e-ddee5ab63f5a",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What does the "
        ],
        [
          "@Entity",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " annotation do in Spring Boot?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8092-88a8-e3ddb52196c5",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "How do you define a primary key in a JPA entity?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-806c-994c-fe7c365919ed",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What is the purpose of "
        ],
        [
          "@GeneratedValue",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", and what strategies are available?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80aa-a56a-c70c1efe6af3",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "How do you implement a one-to-many relationship in JPA?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80cf-a06f-e976b57685c6",
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "What is the difference between lazy and eager loading in JPA?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80ee-a928-e7213419122b",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8042-b720-e0e2285414f4",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "👍 5. Best Practices & Pitfalls"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80ff-8eb5-cfdd4e455582",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "✅ Best Practices"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8020-a1c1-df23de718711",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Always define a primary key ("
        ],
        [
          "@Id",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ") for every entity."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-807d-ad96-dcb881eaea1d",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Use "
        ],
        [
          "cascade = CascadeType.ALL",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " cautiously — only if the child should always follow the parent's lifecycle (e.g., delete children when parent is deleted)."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80b3-898d-f1c2c2480ba7",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Override "
        ],
        [
          "toString()",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " carefully to avoid infinite loops (especially in bidirectional relationships)."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8014-98c2-e0779eee3729",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "❌ Pitfalls"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8034-9ee6-cc4eb87d7609",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Forgetting "
        ],
        [
          "mappedBy",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " in bidirectional relationships — causes duplicate join tables."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80d4-a47f-f6e9e5a5629c",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Using "
        ],
        [
          "EAGER",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " loading by default on large collections — can kill performance."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80f5-a4a1-d3806289f111",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "No-arg constructor missing — JPA needs a public or protected default constructor."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-808b-9e3f-e989346ca64a",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80e2-815d-db0eea3b4e96",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "🔄 6. Connection to Other Concepts"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80a8-9339-e43fd0d8b5ee",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Spring Data JPA",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Once your entities are mapped, Spring Data JPA can auto-generate queries via "
        ],
        [
          "JpaRepository",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8058-b431-de2eb77ce80e",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Service Layer",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Business logic calls repositories to save/fetch these entities."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80b7-9c60-da3f8e849953",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Spring Boot Auto-Configuration",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Automatically sets up JPA if it detects "
        ],
        [
          "spring-boot-starter-data-jpa",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " and a database driver in the classpath."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-807e-bf0a-cf4f451e9e23",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80f9-8a3b-c05c49b92138",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "🗣️ 7. Quick Interview Answers"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80f1-a04e-dd16c656c86a",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: “Can you explain what "
        ],
        [
          "@Entity",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " and "
        ],
        [
          "@Id",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " do in JPA?”"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80fe-bff0-dbeda75a76d9",
      "type": "quote",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Sure! @Entity marks a class as a database-mapped object — meaning each instance will correspond to a row in a table. @Id defines the primary key field for that table, and without it, JPA can’t identify individual records."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8073-97bb-edb12a7c9df5",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: “How would you model a one-to-many relationship between two classes?”"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8084-bd68-fd94805c9025",
      "type": "quote",
      "richText": [
        [
          "I'd use @OneToMany on the parent side and @ManyToOne on the child. For example, a User can have many Posts, so in the User class, I’d have a list of posts with @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"user\"), and in Post, I’d have a User field annotated with @ManyToOne."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-802e-a859-e18bdb9e92c2",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: “What's the difference between lazy and eager loading?”"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80c6-81e1-cdffd0facf6d",
      "type": "quote",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Lazy loading fetches related data only when needed, which is good for performance. Eager loading retrieves everything up front, which might hurt performance if you don’t always need the related data."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80ee-b06c-c43eeed7c184",
      "type": "divider"
    }
  ]
}
